# Vista settings for site/lib/herdr-site.sh.
#
# Install with:
#     mkdir -p ~/.config/herdr-slurm
#     ln -sf ~/herdr/scripts/vista/site.env ~/.config/herdr-slurm/site.env
#
# Inspect what the site layer resolved, and where each value came from:
#     herdr site
#
# Every value here overrides a probe that gets Vista wrong. Nothing in site/ is patched
# to accommodate this cluster; these are its own documented knobs.
#
# Three of Frontera's four overrides are needed here. The fourth is not: see the note on
# cgroups at the bottom.

# The probe (_herdr_site_probe_python) tries /usr/bin/python3.13, .12, .11, then
# python3.13/.12/.11 on PATH, then python3, and gates each on sys.version_info >= (3, 11).
# On Vista every one of those misses: /usr/bin/python3 is 3.9.25 and no python3.1x exists
# anywhere on PATH, so the probe resolves to the empty string and reports
# "unresolved: no python >= 3.11 found".
#
# Vista DOES have a python3/3.11.8 Lmod module, but it cannot be named here directly:
# its binary is /opt/apps/gcc14/cuda12/python3/3.11.8/bin/python3.11 and it does not run
# without its module's lib dir on LD_LIBRARY_PATH --
#     libpython3.11.so.1.0 => not found
# even with a completely scrubbed environment. Pointing HERDR_SITE_PYTHON at it yields
# exit 127 inside the sidebar provider.
#
# So this points at a relocatable python-build-standalone 3.12.13 unpacked under
# $WORK instead. Verified to run the provider's imports under the exact flags
# site/config/scripts/herdr-jobs uses:
#     env -i HOME=$HOME PATH=/usr/bin:/bin .../python3 -I -S -c 'import json,os,subprocess...'
# That keeps Vista free of Frontera's python3-frontera.sh wrapper: there is no wrapper
# script here, and there should not be one.
HERDR_SITE_PYTHON=/work/08526/jdgeorga/vista/herdr-build/python/bin/python3

# The login-node probe (_herdr_site_probe_login_prefix) derives a prefix from
# `hostname -s` by stripping from the first digit. That is right on a login node and
# wrong everywhere else: run from Vista compute node i614-013 it yields prefix "i" and
# pattern "^i[0-9]+$", so every real login node is rejected as not belonging to this
# cluster. Vista usage is specifically FROM a compute node, so both values are pinned.
#
# Set BOTH. LOGIN_PATTERN is probed independently of LOGIN_PREFIX, so overriding the
# prefix alone leaves a stale pattern and the override silently does nothing.
#
# Vista has exactly two login nodes, login1 and login2 (login3/login4 do not resolve in
# DNS). Note that vista.tacc.utexas.edu is NOT either of them: it round-robins two
# separate addresses, so `hostname -s` after login is login1 or login2, which is what
# this pattern must match.
HERDR_SITE_LOGIN_PREFIX=login
HERDR_SITE_LOGIN_PATTERN=^login[0-9]+$

# The default fork binary path is $REPO/target/release/herdr. On Vista the build tree
# lives on $WORK instead, because $HOME is NFS with a 500k FILE quota (137k already used)
# and only ~12.6G free of a 23.8G space quota, while a rustup toolchain plus a release
# target dir is tens of thousands of files and several GB. CARGO_TARGET_DIR is set to
# match by scripts/vista/env.sh.
#
# This must be pinned even though _herdr_site_probe_fork_bin checks CARGO_TARGET_DIR
# first: that probe only fires when CARGO_TARGET_DIR is already exported, which is true
# in a build shell that sourced env.sh and false for every ordinary `herdr` invocation.
HERDR_SITE_FORK_BIN=/work/08526/jdgeorga/vista/herdr-build/target/release/herdr

# NOT set, deliberately: HERDR_SITE_CGROUP_MODE and HERDR_SITE_CGROUP_DIR.
#
# Frontera needs these because it is cgroup v1 with an unreadable per-user slice, which
# makes `herdr health` print zeroed columns -- worse than no check at all. Vista is
# cgroup v2 (/sys/fs/cgroup is cgroup2fs, cgroup.controllers exists), so the probe's v2
# branch fires and resolves /sys/fs/cgroup/user.slice/user-878254.slice, which is real
# and readable. Measured there: memory.current 6670843904, pids.current 28,
# pids.max 160525. The probe is correct on Vista; overriding it would only add a value
# that can go stale. See README.md, "herdr health actually works here".
